I have a very complicated relationship  with death as a medical student I've  learned about death I've been with  patients as they've died and I can even  talk about death after all I did title  my talk let's talk about death but in my  heart I don't accept death  I find it excruciating death just seems  so unfair  so out of my control so final ever since  I was little I've had this horrible fear  that I would die and not have a chance  to say goodbye after my grandmother  passed away when I was 13 I started  writing a ton of notes to everyone I  knew to say goodbye with the idea that  maybe somehow they would be sent out in  the event that I met an unexpected end I  pictured death by lightning I was a  pretty dramatic child but I figured if I  couldn't control death then at the very  least I could try to make sure that my  final actions were according to what I  valued making sure that the people I  love feel loved now fast forward to my  training at the hospital I'd like to  tell you about a patient let's call him  Sam Sam was fantastic he was 78 years  old he had a huge family and he loved  telling me stories about his old surfing  days Sam had cancer and kidney disease  and blood clots he could no longer move  on his own when I met Sam he had been  brought in to the hospital by an  ambulance with internal bleeding Sam was  in a lot of pain but even despite that  every time I would walk into his room he  would look at me and say hey baby how  you doing  grandpa still had game unfortunately  though his situation quickly became  worse as his medical team we decided to  broach the topic of death we asked him  what would you want Sam in case your  heart stops beating or you stopped  breathing Sam would have nothing of it  Sam got angry and his family got angry  and frankly I got pretty angry at the  situation - but we had reached medicines  limits Sam ultimately went home with no  plan he ended up coming back several  times to the hospital until he finally  passed away at the hospital in a cold  sterile environment surrounded by  machines I've often wondered what would  Sam's final year have looked like how do  you tried hospice Hospice is a service  for the dying which optimizes for  comfort and symptom management rather  than focusing on trying to find one more  cure the research tells us that for  folks who try hospice they often live  longer and more peaceful lives than  those who don't I don't know what Sam  would have wanted we never talked about  death but what I do know is that Sam had  never thought about death and that's the  case for for most of us and what that  means is that when we get to the point  where we can more or less anticipate  death the time that we have left becomes  more about death and less about life by  show of hand how many of us are  uncomfortable talking or planning for  death me too  we don't think about death we don't talk  about death we don't plan for death so  I'd like to challenge us all today to  change our conversation to change our  culture to include death because when we  plan for a meaningful death not only do  we die better but we also live better so  before we move on I'd like to lay out a  few basic facts to put us all on the  same page number one until Google comes  up with a cure we're all going to die  number two god forbid that somebody  actually gets struck by lightning but  the majority of us will die of terminal  illness or old age so let's focus on the  latter two and number three there's no  wrong choice when it comes to how you  want to spend the end of life but  oftentimes it is a choice modern  medicine has made tremendous advances  but it's also convinced us that maybe we  can cure death we can't so instead the  conversation becomes how do we want to  die we spend our whole lives planning  for our milestones writing our  narratives planning for that big job or  the wedding or even a low-key notes  talk but yet the final chapter still  sneaks up on us in the u.s. most of us  don't die the way we want to while the  majority of elderly folks have indicated  that they'd like to die at home only 25%  actually do in medicine our default is  to throw a ton of interventions at you  in an effort to prolong life but that  often comes at the cost of quality time  and spending your last time perhaps the  way you want the details of why we die  are different for everyone but if we set  our goals ahead of time then our doctors  can help us figure out which  interventions will help us meet those  goals I'd like to tell you about a  community and La Crosse Wisconsin they  it's a town in which they had a huge  widespread initiative to get everyone to  write their advanced  or the documents which you can specify  what you want at the end of life they  change their culture they change their  conversation they even change their  healthcare system to adjust to the need  and the result 96% of people had an  advanced directive on file when they  died and most people tried to most  people wanted to die at home and that's  how they died on their own terms for me  the most important reason why I care so  deeply about this is because of the  impact that it could have on my family  when we specify how we want to die when  we set our wishes forth we take the  burden of choice away from our families  the research tells us that those who  have a plan in place their families  suffer less stress less anxiety less  depression and they're free to breathe  rather than second-guess their judgement  planning for death will never take away  grief grief is inevitable but  second-guessing is not so let's change  our conversation let's think about death  let's plan for death let's talk about  death step 1  thinking about death in medicine were  taught to ask several questions when we  start off an end-of-life discussion  questions such as what matters most to  you what are your hopes what are your  goals and what are you willing to trade  off for them you might recognize these  questions as the very same ones that we  use when we think about life in fact the  career office has given us a fantastic  resource to help guide this that bet  you'll never look at that the same way  again  step2 talk about death sit down with  your parents your grandparents your  friends anybody ask them how do they  want to die and tell them what you want  as the Business School has taught us  maybe role play it out first and you  know we won't know all of the details  but if we paint the broad strokes early  then we'll be able to fill in those  details better later again there are a  few good resources that can help guide  this so check out the conversation  project death with dinner or read Atul  Gawande book being mortal and finally  step 3 write your advance directives  change them over time and tell your  family your friends and and and your  doctors about this and even if you don't  write an advance directive at the very  least make sure that you designate a  proxy and everyone knows about it  planning for death will never take away  the pain of loss the pain of losing  someone who's close to us and not having  them with us anymore I'm not sure that  I'll ever be okay with death I'll never  stop missing my grandma or Sam or the  countless other people whose hands I've  had the privilege of holding but I  believe that if we plan for a meaningful  death we can die the way that we lived  on our own terms because life is so  precious so let's talk about death  because I'm planning for a meaningful  death we allow ourselves to lead a  meaningful life
 

